course: dictionary
lesson: 9027
title: Slack: loose, quiet or lazy?
level: primary-5
stars: 3
explain: One word can have several meanings, and the context tells you which one you need. Look at the words around slack. «Short, thin, tight strings wobble fast and sound high; long, thick, slack strings wobble slowly and sound low.» Slack is the opposite of tight, so it means loose, not pulled tight.
explain: Test your meaning by swapping it into the sentence. «A string telephone works because a tight string carries the wobble from one cup to the other; a slack string or a finger on it stops the sound.» A loose string cannot carry the wobble, so the sentence still makes sense.
explain: Other meanings of slack depend on the clues around them. «Don't slack off before the test» means work less hard than you should. «Give him some slack, he's new» means be patient with him. The clues show you which meaning the writer wants.
fact: Context means the words and sentences around a word.
fact: «Slack» means loose, not pulled tight: a slack rope.
fact: «Slack» can mean not busy, as in business is slack in winter, or lazy and careless, as in slack work.
fact: To slack off means to work less hard than you should.
fact: «Slacks» are casual trousers, and the word is always plural.
exercise: «Short, thin, tight strings wobble fast and sound high; long, thick, slack strings wobble slowly and sound low.» What does slack mean in this sentence? | *Loose, not pulled tight | Lazy and careless | Very thick and heavy | Not busy
exercise: «A string telephone works because a tight string carries the wobble from one cup to the other; a slack string or a finger on it stops the sound.» Find and copy one word that means the opposite of tight. | cup | *slack | string | telephone
exercise: Ms Patel says, «Don't slack off before the test.» What does slack off mean? | Wear smart trousers | Stretch a rope tight | Go to the shop for a snack | *Work less hard than you should
test: Ben says, «Give him some slack, he's new.» What does this ask you to do? | Tell him off for being late | Ignore him completely | *Be more patient and forgiving with him | Pull the rope tight
test: «Business is slack in winter.» What does slack mean here? | Loose and wobbly | Very strong | *Not busy | Happy and bright
exam: Which sentence uses slack to mean loose, not pulled tight? | Sam wore his slacks to school | Ravi said, «Cut me some slack», meaning be kinder | *A slack rope hung between the two poles | Business was slack in the shop that week
